---
id: org.dxos.plugin.blocks
name: BlocksPlugin
version: 0.1.0
---

A block-based outliner where every bullet is a first-class ECHO object. Designed to losslessly absorb Tana Paste imports.

## Rules

```mdl
rule R-Edges-First-Class:
  desc: |
    Structural parent/child links between Blocks are modelled as
    first-class ECHO `Relation` entities (the `ChildEdge` schema in
    `types/ChildEdge.ts`) — NOT as opaque entries in
    `Block.children: Ref<Block>[]`. The relation carries `source`,
    `target`, `order`, and reserves room for per-occurrence
    `expanded` and a future `kind` taxonomy.

    Why: a tree where `Block.children` is the only parent/child
    representation forces single-parent-per-Block and offers
    nowhere to attach per-edge data (ordering across multiple
    parents, per-occurrence collapse, provenance, edge labels for
    'structural' vs 'embed' vs 'linked'). Materialising the edge as
    an entity unlocks a property-graph model on top of ECHO without
    touching `@dxos/echo` (per rule R-No-Echo-Changes).

    How to apply: new structural-children writers MUST go through
    `createChildEdge(db, parent, child)` from
    `components/BlockNode/child-edges.ts`. Readers MUST use
    `useStructuralChildren(parent)` (or its sync sibling
    `getStructuralChildren`) which merges `Block.children` (legacy,
    still authoritative for outline bullets that haven't been
    ported) with ChildEdges fanning out of the parent. Direct reads
    of `block.children` are deprecated and survive only until each
    call-site is migrated. New domain relationships beyond
    parent/child (e.g. WorksAt, AssignedTo, Wraps) follow the same
    pattern: define a `Type.relation(...)` schema in `types/`,
    register it in `BlocksPlugin`, write helpers + a hook.
```

```mdl
rule R-No-Echo-Changes:
  desc: |
    plugin-blocks is a strict consumer of `@dxos/echo` and its sibling
    packages (`@dxos/types`, `@dxos/schema`, etc.). It MUST NOT depend
    on edits to those packages, schema additions there, or any new
    ECHO API. Everything plugin-blocks needs — including Tana-style
    behaviours such as materializing enum choices as Blocks — must be
    implemented as a layer ABOVE the ECHO surface that ships today.

    Why: keeps the plugin shippable independently of core ECHO
    release cycles, prevents the outliner from accidentally
    co-evolving with the substrate, and forces design choices that
    respect ECHO's stable API as the contract.

    How to apply: when an F-* increment seems to need a schema field,
    annotation, or helper that doesn't exist in `@dxos/echo` today,
    re-shape the feature so it lives inside plugin-blocks (e.g.,
    extra Blocks acting as registries, plugin-private metadata on the
    Block's `content`, lookups keyed by typename+fieldName+value).
    Never reach for a `@dxos/types` schema edit, a new ECHO
    annotation, or a new core helper as the path of least resistance.
```

